These target the inode read-storm far more directly than my (misdirected) dir-level fix. Let me check their defaults + meaning (cluster-free):
Now I see a **much stronger lead**. `inode_cluster_owned_skip` (default on) skips re-staling inode clusters only when **AG-owned**. The shared inodes (root, .dlm_scaling) live in **AG0, owned only by node-slot-0 (test1)** — so the **other 31 nodes DON'T skip → they re-stale + cold-re-read the shared AG0 inode clusters every access → the storm!** The safe fix is the inode analog of the GFS2 principle: skip when the **inode is held ≥PR** (DLM-lock-protected → no peer can have modified), not just when AG-owned. `fua_skip_owned_inode` is the unsafe (AG-based) version. Let me find the exact inode-cluster re-read/re-stale site:
